[SEVERITY: CRITICAL] — Unencrypted UUID in Public Assessment Endpoint Enables Unauthorized Access
What's wrong:
The PublicAssessmentResolver.getFullPublicAssessment() query accepts a UUID and returns FullPublicAssessment without ANY authentication or authorization guards. The UUID is generated with uuidv4() which, while random, is transmitted in plaintext emails and embedded in URLs. There is no rate limiting, expiration check, or additional validation on this endpoint.

Where exactly:

File: src/modules/assessment/resolvers/public.assesment.resolver.ts
Line(s): 10-15
Function/Component: getFullPublicAssessment()
Why it matters:
UUIDs are not secrets—they're identifiers. If any assessment UUID is leaked (via email forwarding, browser history, logs), anyone with that UUID can access the full assessment data including patient questionnaire responses. This is a direct HIPAA/GDPR violation for PHI access.

Fix:
Add time-limited, single-use tokens with HMAC signatures:

typescript
// Generate secure token with expiration
@Query(() => FullPublicAssessment)
async getFullPublicAssessment(
    @Args('token', { type: () => String }) token: string,
): Promise<FullPublicAssessment> {
    const { uuid, exp, sig } = this.verifySignedToken(token);
    if (Date.now() > exp) throw new UnauthorizedException('Token expired');
    
    // Additional rate limiting and audit logging required
    return this.assessmentService.getFullPublicAssessment(uuid);
}
[SEVERITY: HIGH] — Race Condition in Assessment Creation Allows Duplicate MongoDB Records
What's wrong:
In AssessmentService.createNewAssessment(), a MongoDB questionnaire assessment is created first (line 166), then a PostgreSQL assessment is created in a try-catch (lines 174-241). If the PostgreSQL save fails after the MongoDB creation, the catch block attempts to remove the MongoDB record—but this is not atomic. Network failures, process crashes, or database errors between these operations leave orphaned MongoDB records.

Where exactly:

File: src/modules/assessment/services/assessment.service.ts
Line(s): 149-244
Function/Component: createNewAssessment()
Why it matters:
Orphaned questionnaire assessments accumulate in MongoDB, consuming storage and potentially leaking sensitive questionnaire data even after PostgreSQL records are "cleaned up." This creates a data consistency nightmare and potential compliance issue.

Fix:
Implement the Saga pattern or use a distributed transaction coordinator:

typescript
// Use MongoDB sessions with retryable writes
async createNewAssessment(input) {
    const session = await this.mongoConnection.startSession();
    session.startTransaction();
    
    try {
        const mongoAssessment = await this.questionnaireAssessmentService
            .createNewAssessment(input.questionnaires, { session });
        
        const pgAssessment = await this.assessmentRepository.save({
            questionnaireAssessmentId: mongoAssessment.id,
            // ... other fields
        });
        
        await session.commitTransaction();
        return pgAssessment;
    } catch (err) {
        await session.abortTransaction();
        throw err;
    } finally {
        session.endSession();
    }
}
