[SEVERITY: HIGH] — Email Content Vulnerable to Template Injection via Handlebars
What's wrong:
The SendMailService.sendEmail() method uses Handlebars to compile email templates with user-controlled data. The templateData object includes a link field generated from user-controlled assessment data (UUID), and the template body comes from the database without validation. Handlebars has known prototype pollution and code execution vulnerabilities when compiling untrusted templates.

Where exactly:

File: src/modules/mail/services/send-mail.service.ts
Line(s): 84-106
Function/Component: sendEmail()
Why it matters:
A malicious user with template management permissions could inject Handlebars helpers that execute arbitrary code on the server or leak environment variables. This is Remote Code Execution (RCE) via template injection.

Fix:
Sanitize template inputs and use a sandboxed template engine:

typescript
// Use handlebars with strict mode and no escapeHtml
import * as handlebars from 'handlebars';
// Pre-compile templates with strict mode
const template = handlebars.compile(mailTemplate.body, {
    noEscape: false,
    strict: true,
    preventIndent: true
});
// Whitelist allowed variables
const allowedVars = ['link', 'patientName'];
const sanitizedData = Object.keys(templateData)
    .filter(k => allowedVars.includes(k))
    .reduce((acc, k) => ({ ...acc, [k]: templateData[k] }), {});
[SEVERITY: HIGH] — Argon2 Weak Configuration (Memory Cost Too Low)
What's wrong:
The Argon2 configuration in hash.helper.ts uses a memory cost of only 1024 KB (1 MB). The current OWASP recommendation for Argon2id is minimum 19 MB (19456 KB) of memory. The current configuration is approximately 19x weaker than recommended and vulnerable to GPU/ASIC cracking.

Where exactly:

File: src/shared/helpers/hash.helper.ts
Line(s): 47-57
Function/Component: argon2Hash()
Why it matters:
Patient data, assessment responses, and authentication credentials are protected by passwords hashed with weak parameters. A data breach would allow attackers to crack passwords orders of magnitude faster than with properly configured Argon2.

Fix:
Update Argon2 parameters to OWASP 2023 recommendations:

typescript
// src/shared/helpers/hash.helper.ts lines 47-57
static async argon2Hash(value: string): Promise<string> {
    const argon2Config = {
        type: argon2.argon2id,
        memoryCost: 19456,  // 19 MB minimum per OWASP
        parallelism: 2,
        timeCost: 2,
        hashLength: 32,
        saltLength: 16,
    };
    return argon2.hash(value, argon2Config);
}
